00:09:50.730 --> 00:09:52.660
And then what they
asked me was, first,

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:54.700
they asked me some
C++ related questions.

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:56.530
Oh, what's object
oriented programming

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:59.170
and stuff, inheritance,
and stuff like that.

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.120
And then they asked
me some linked lists

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:03.400
questions I remember I think.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:06.100
And then they also asked
me a binary search problem.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:08.560
For example, can you
use binary search

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:12.370
to find if a number exists?

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:16.570
I think they give you an
array of assorted number.

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:20.590
So first, the easiest solution
would be just go linearly.

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:25.770
But that would be you have
to know your time complexity

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:26.930
stuff.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:29.924
So then first to brute force
ways is just scan it linearly.

00:10:29.924 --> 00:10:32.090
And then later on they say
can you do it any better?

00:10:32.090 --> 00:10:35.170
And then that's where you put
out the binary search method.

00:10:35.170 --> 00:10:37.030
And then I think that's it.

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:39.790
That's the hardest
questions there.

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:40.809
So it was pretty simple.

00:10:40.809 --> 00:10:42.100
Because it's a finance company.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:43.308
They don't ask you hard shit.

00:10:43.308 --> 00:10:45.790
